parts.append(section('03','iteration','The loop you repeat all day.','Developers and agents usually change a small part of a site. Iteration speed often matters more than cold full-build speed.',
'''<div class="spotlight"><div><strong>1.31s</strong><span>deno / unchanged publication median</span><p>Verified derived output and dependency-aware composition make a no-change publication inexpensive.</p></div><div><strong>4–6s</strong><span>deno / common body-edit observations</span><p>One, ten and one hundred authored-body edits complete in 4.48s, 4.91s and 5.61s. This shorthand describes those observations, not a latency guarantee.</p></div></div>
<p>A short feedback loop compounds over dozens or hundreds of edits: build, inspect, correct, repeat. These measurements cover publication until output exists. Source editing, coordinated agent projection updates, browser inspection and review are outside timed builds; end-to-end productivity was not measured.</p><div class="data-block">'''+t+'''</div>
<div class="note"><strong>Correctness travels with incremental speed.</strong><p>Dependency-aware rebuilds are a core architectural advantage, but stale route/download/search/alias retirement matters just as much. Every case above was compared with forced recomputation; corruption and metadata-removal probes provide additional checks.</p><p>Shared-source cases expose remaining costs: navigation, metadata and reference changes invalidate broadly; changed retrieval inputs trigger whole-index search preparation. Those slower rows remain visible.</p></div>
<p><strong>Upstream changed-input production observation: 135.08s total.</strong> This single observation repeats reference generation, search and full Lume production publication. It is <strong>not Lume development-server hot reload</strong>. Lume’s development-server latency was not measured and no claim is made about it.</p>'''))
