parts.append(section('04','benchmarks','Measure the work, not just the winner.','Cached publication, forced rendering and upstream reference generation are different workloads. Each boundary stays attached to its numbers.',
warm_chart+'<div class="data-block">'+dual_table()+'''</div><div class="note"><p><strong>Unchanged</strong> can reuse verified derived pages/exports. <strong>Forced full</strong> bypasses human caches and forces Nift composition. <strong>Fresh application state</strong> clears generated/output/build metadata while retaining committed inputs and installed dependencies. OS caches are uncontrolled; this is not a machine-cold test.</p><p>The final fresh human median being lower than forced warm is retained as observed. Active-host/runtime/OS variation is uncontrolled, not a demonstrated advantage of cold state.</p></div>
<h3>Upstream production workflow</h3>'''+nt+'''<p class="small">Frozen live std inputs; exact pinned WASM delivery. Compilation/startup remain measured. Full native includes reference type/doc regeneration → local search → Lume publication. Prepared-input native omits reference regeneration, matching the migration’s prepared-reference ownership more closely.</p>
<div class="note"><strong>Original official baseline: one observation, not a median.</strong><p>The original complete <code>deno task build</code> was <strong>171.56s / 3,117.1 MiB</strong>. It remains separate from the repeated campaign, whose live std acquisition and WASM delivery boundaries differ.</p><p>Native warm Lume already caches unchanged OG images by JSX. Native application-cold publication regenerates them. The migrations copy maintained images and frontend assets, so these are architecture comparisons, not identical transformation workloads.</p></div>'''))
history=f'''<div class="history"><article><p class="eyebrow">01 / Initial architecture</p><strong>134.86s / 144.07s</strong><p>Warm medians, deno / deno-agent. Both unnecessarily regenerated all 834 OG images during routine builds.</p><small>Historical five-run migration evidence retained.</small></article><article><p class="eyebrow">02 / Static-asset correction</p><strong>23.35s / 1.54s</strong><p>First corrected individual observations. Accepted image bytes became maintained assets, copied at their original public URLs.</p><small>Single observations, not medians.</small></article><article><p class="eyebrow">03 / Profiled architecture</p><strong>13.16s / 1.07s</strong><p>Final forced-full medians. Shared work, verified caches and persistent DOM workers reduce avoidable rendering cost.</p><small>Intermediate and earlier optimized campaigns stay separate.</small></article></div>'''
parts.append(section('05','image-correction','Stop rebuilding what you maintain.','The OG-image correction changed the benchmark architecture because it corrected the intended ownership model.',
history+'''<p>The initial routine regeneration was an implementation mistake. The finished site needs the accepted image bytes; the migrations maintain all 834 images under <code>assets/og/</code> and publish them at their original URLs. Regenerating an image after a page/title/template change is an <strong>explicit selected-image maintenance operation</strong>, not a hidden step in every build.</p><pre tabindex="0" aria-label="Explicit image maintenance"><code>python3 scripts/update-og.py --route /runtime/run/</code></pre>
<p>Upstream Lume’s image generation remains in its production pipeline and cold measurements. Moving generation into intentional source maintenance is not a claim that image updates are free; their diagnostic costs and unaffected-image checks are retained separately.</p>
<details><summary>Retained intermediate evidence and campaign variation</summary><p>The first optimized five-sample campaign measured deno forced/fresh at 16.51s / 16.70s and deno-agent at 1.28s / 1.45s. Final samples were collected after a small layout-removal correctness fix. That fix is not evidence of the later timing improvement: host, runtime and OS-cache variation remain uncontrolled. Both campaigns, intermediate cache evidence and the earlier human cold RSS outlier of 2,225.8 MiB remain recorded.</p></details>'''))
